  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 225 PM Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Cold air damming and breezy/cool weather will \npersist through Thu. Dry weather and increasing sunshine will follow \nfor Fri through the middle of next week, with temps returning to \nabove normal this weekend. \n\nWe're currently in the throes of a robust, classical cold air \ndamming event, featuring a strong parent high (~1036 mb central \npressure) anchored over S Quebec nosing southward down through \ncentral NC, with steady overrunning rain and drizzle over most of \nthe area. NW Piedmont soundings (both 12z observed and RAP \nforecasts) show saturation and high stability throughout the lowest \n1-1.5 km, with a tight MSLP gradient and advection of cool/dense air \nresulting in a stout NE wind with periodic gusts over 20 kts. This \nwedge regime, blocked by the terrain to our W and by the remnant \nfrontal zone to our S and E, will be maintained by this advection of \ncool and low-dewpoint air through Thu, despite a very gradual \ninfiltration of more deeply drier air through the 1-2 km into our \narea from the NE and subsequent trend of diminishing and \nincreasingly shallow moist upglide. Rain/drizzle chances should \nremain fairly high areawide into this evening, then persist mainly \nacross our W and S later tonight through much of Thu, as a series of \nperturbations aloft (emanating from the mid level low over the Ohio \nValley) tracks across the western Carolinas. Temps today will \nstruggle to reach highs in the low-mid 60s over the Piedmont and mid \n60s to around 70 across our far S and E, and highs should be \nsimilarly cool Thu as clouds stay locked in for much of the day \nareawide. Expect highs Thu to range from low 60s NW to the low 70s \nin the far SE, which are about 10-15 deg F below the normal highs \nfor this time of year. We're still seeing a high potential for lows \nin the mid-upper 40s over the NW Piedmont Thu night and again Fri \nnight.\n\nBy Fri, the slow-moving Ohio Valley mid level low will have shifted \nover NC and be pushing to our E, as much lower PW spreads into \ncentral NC from the N and NNW. The more deeply drier air and loss of \ndynamic forcing for ascent will lead to increasing sunshine overall, \nalthough clouds may linger for a longer time across our northeast \nFri, courtesy of the developing intense low just off the Mid \nAtlantic coast. As this low wobbles and drifts slowly northward off \nthe Northeast coast through the weekend, the center of the weakening \nparent high will retrograde WSW into the central Great Lakes but \ncontinue to extend into our area. This will equate to generally fair \nskies, dry weather, and a warming trend, from below normal temps Fri \nto near normal readings by Sat and above normal readings for Sun \nthrough Wed. Highs should be back into the 80s Tue/Wed, although \nafternoon dewpoints in the 50s to around 60s should keep conditions \nfrom becoming too uncomfortable.",
